WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.170
One country does not own a single warship; all it has is a few coast guard boats and a steel cutter towed behind them.

2
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.980
Facing it is one of the most powerful navies in the world.

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:18.950
Eighteen years later the side that walks away from the table will be Iceland, and today the sea borders of the entire world run along the line it drew.

4
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:33.520
Picture an island sitting in the middle of the North Atlantic, in the cold emptiness between Greenland and Scotland.

5
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:41.170
Its surface area is that of a mid-sized European country, but most of its land is glacier, lava field and barren highland.

6
00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.020
There are almost no trees.

7
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.070
Grain will not grow.

8
00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:50.200
Beneath the ground there is no coal, no iron, no oil.

9
00:00:50.200 --> 00:01:01.050
In the middle of the nineteen seventies, the number of people living on this island is roughly two hundred and twenty thousand — the population of a medium-sized district, and it has built itself a state,

10
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.820
with its own flag, its own parliament and its own foreign ministry.

11
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.790
The only thing Iceland possessed was the water around it.

12
00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:13.760
And that water carried one of the most fertile fishing grounds on earth.

13
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:24.130
The cold current descending from the north met the warm Atlantic water coming up from the south over the shallow continental shelf that rings the island; that meeting set off an explosion of plankton,

14
00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.740
the plankton fed the small fish, and the small fish fed the cod.

15
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:37.350
The cod was the most patient hunter of cold water: slow-growing, long-lived, white-fleshed, and suited to salting, drying and freezing.

16
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.800
For centuries it carried the silent backbone of Europe's protein supply.

17
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.930
The entire Icelandic economy was built on that single item.

18
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:54.860
From the end of the nineteenth century to the middle of the nineteen seventies, fish and fish products made up eighty to ninety percent of the country's total exports.

19
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.630
To put that plainly: between eighty and ninety percent of everything the country sold abroad.

20
00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.440
For an economy, that is an unthinkable fragility.

21
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.690
In an industrial country, if one sector collapses, the others carry the weight.

22
00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.860
In Iceland there was no other sector to carry it.

23
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:18.550
If the price of fish fell, wages fell; if the shoals thinned, the country could not service its debt.

24
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:25.400
The most important document on a minister's desk in Reykjavik was not the budget — it was the catch statistics.

25
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.490
The problem was this: in law, those waters did not belong to Iceland.

26
00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:39.260
Until it declared full independence from Denmark in nineteen forty-four, the island was subject to the Danish crown and could not sign treaties in its own name.

27
00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:46.590
A convention signed between Denmark and Britain in nineteen oh one had drawn the fishing limit off Iceland's coasts at three miles.

28
00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:52.160
Three nautical miles — roughly five and a half kilometres.

29
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.090
To the eye of a shepherd looking out from the shore, that is far short of the horizon.

30
00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.420
Everything beyond that line was open to everyone.

31
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.550
And beyond that line, a fleet was waiting.

32
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:13.000
Britain's distant-water trawling fleet had been sending steam, and later diesel, trawlers to fish the Icelandic banks since the end of the nineteenth century.

33
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:21.130
These vessels sailed from the ports of Hull, Grimsby and Fleetwood, stayed at sea for three weeks, filled their holds with ice and came home.

34
00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:33.740
In Hull, fishing was not a trade but an identity: a father taught his son how to mend a trawl net, in what weather the hatch had to be closed, what kind of wound a rope frozen in the Arctic Ocean opened in a man's hand.

35
00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:38.350
In certain neighbourhoods of the city, almost every house had a man at sea.

36
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:45.520
These people were not going to Iceland as invaders; they were going where their fathers had gone, and the law stood behind them.

37
00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:50.290
Both sides were right, and that is precisely why agreement was impossible.

38
00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:58.420
For the British fisherman, the Icelandic banks were a field that had been worked for three generations, and closing it was the theft of his livelihood.

39
00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:06.390
For the Icelander, those same waters were the country's only income, and every year the foreign trawlers came bigger, stronger and hungrier.

40
00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:15.800
In the nineteen fifties, when modern trawling technology combined with the sonar and radar developed during the war, catching fish turned into something else entirely.

41
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:20.730
Finding the fish was no longer a matter of experience but of instruments.

42
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:28.220
And the bottom trawl, sweeping the seabed, carried away not only the fish but the ground on which the fish bred.

43
00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.950
Icelandic scientists began measuring the decline of the cod stock in the nineteen fifties.

44
00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:37.440
The figures said that everything in the sea was exhaustible.

45
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.730
For the government in Reykjavik, this was not an environmental question.

46
00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.900
It was a question of existence.

47
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.230
If the fish ended, the state ended.

48
00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:55.240
That sentence was not rhetorical exaggeration but arithmetic you could read straight off the budget table.

49
00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:02.570
And when a state truly believes its existence is under threat, it becomes ready to use every instrument it has.

50
00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.780
Iceland had no army.

51
00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.150
It had never had a navy.

52
00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:12.240
But it had something else: the bargaining power of a side with nothing left to lose.

53
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:18.450
In nineteen forty-nine, Iceland served notice that it was terminating the nineteen oh one convention.

54
00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:26.100
The agreement expired in nineteen fifty-one, and for the first time the island was in a position to draw the limits of its own waters.

55
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:34.550
On the fifteenth of May, nineteen fifty-two, Reykjavik took the first step: the fishing limit was pushed out from three miles to four.

56
00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:36.760
The figure looks small.

57
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.450
But the figure was never the real issue.

58
00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:46.940
With the same decision Iceland declared straight baselines — instead of tracing every indentation of the coast one by one,

59
00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:53.470
it joined the tips of its headlands with straight lines and declared the bays and fjords in between to be internal waters.

60
00:05:53.470 --> 00:06:02.080
So what looked on the map like an expansion of a single mile was, in practice, closing off the shallow bays where the trawlers fished most productively.

61
00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.570
Britain's answer was not diplomatic but commercial.

62
00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:11.300
The trawler owners organized and blocked Icelandic fish from being landed in British ports.

63
00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:18.310
The embargo lasted four years and struck Iceland at its most vulnerable point: it had lost its biggest customer.

64
00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:22.440
But the result was the exact opposite of what London had calculated.

65
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.050
Reykjavik found a new buyer, and that buyer was the Soviet Union.

66
00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:34.380
In the tensest years of the Cold War, the critical island on NATO's northern flank began selling its frozen cod to Moscow.

67
00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:40.910
For the Western alliance, this was the first sign that a quarrel over fish could open into a strategic fracture.

68
00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:52.240
The embargo was quietly relaxed in nineteen fifty-six, and Iceland had learned its lesson: it was not a country that could be pressured, because it had already diversified the market it stood to lose.

69
00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.210
The real crisis came two years later.

70
00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:04.020
Iceland announced that from the first of September, nineteen fifty-eight, it was extending its fishing limit to twelve miles.

71
00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:11.350
That went far beyond what international law generally accepted at the time, and Britain declared that it would not recognize it.

72
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:17.400
And it did not stop at declarations: it sent the Royal Navy to protect its trawler fleet.

73
00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:24.570
Over the period known as the First Cod War, fifty-three British warships served in Icelandic waters at one time or another.

74
00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:28.220
Destroyers, frigates, supply ships.

75
00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:31.070
And on the other side?

76
00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:35.680
A handful of coast guard vessels, barely more than the fingers of one hand.

77
00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:42.370
These were not warships; they were patrol boats armed with one or two small guns and crewed by thirty or forty people.

78
00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:46.020
Next to a frigate they looked like a seal beside a whale.

79
00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:54.950
Britain gathered its trawlers into designated areas called boxes, the warships patrolled around those boxes, and the trawlers shot their nets under protection.

80
00:07:54.950 --> 00:08:05.000
The Icelandic Coast Guard, for its part, tried to come alongside any foreign trawler it could catch, put a boarding party aboard and tow the ship into port.

81
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:14.250
Now and then warning shots were fired; now and then Icelandic officers climbed onto a trawler's deck only to be stranded there when a British warship closed in.

82
00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:18.540
For three years the tension climbed, but nobody died.

83
00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.470
In the spring of nineteen sixty-one the two sides reached a settlement.

84
00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:28.720
Britain accepted that it would no longer contest Iceland's twelve-mile fishing zone.

85
00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:36.850
In return, a three-year transition was granted: in designated areas, in designated months, British trawlers could go on fishing.

86
00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:48.020
And a clause was written into the agreement; if Iceland should ever seek to extend its limits again and one of the parties objected, the matter would be taken to the International Court of Justice.

87
00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:51.030
London saw that clause as a guarantee.

88
00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:56.600
There would be no more unilateral extension, and if there were, the law would step in.

89
00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:02.810
Reykjavik, reading the same clause, saw something different: twelve miles had been won.

90
00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:06.140
What has been won once can be won again.

91
00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:16.670
Because what really decided matters was not the legal text but the fact the decade had demonstrated; one of the greatest navies in the world had not been able to tear a handful of patrol boats out of the sea.

92
00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:20.960
Britain could protect its trawlers, but it could not make Iceland give up.

93
00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.130
Those two things were not the same thing.

94
00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:28.100
And Iceland had understood that the second one mattered more.

95
00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:33.510
In an asymmetric struggle, the weaker side has to do something the stronger side cannot.

96
00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.840
The answer Iceland found was not a weapon.

97
00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:40.650
It was a tool — a kind of shears, in fact.

98
00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:49.900
The idea belonged to the Coast Guard's commander, Pétur Sigurdsson; the design was worked out together with two craftsmen who knew their way around ironwork.

99
00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:52.590
The way it worked was startlingly simple.

100
00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:59.920
A heavy steel piece fitted with a hook was let out over the ship's stern on a long line and dragged along at a certain depth below the surface.

101
00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:05.650
The patrol vessel would pass astern of a fishing trawler, across the steel warps towing its net.

102
00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:11.700
The dragging hook would catch a warp, the taut steel would enter the jaws of the cutter, and it would part.

103
00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:19.030
What a parted warp meant was this: the trawl net, its doors, its chain and the catch inside it all went to the sea floor.

104
00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:25.720
A set of trawl gear in those years was an investment of thousands of pounds, and a spare was not always carried aboard.

105
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:35.130
A trawler that lost its net could not fish; a trawler that could not fish went home empty, after coming fifteen hundred miles, burning fuel and paying its crew.

106
00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:40.700
A single cut, a single movement of the hand, turned that voyage into a loss.

107
00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:44.510
Nobody was hurt, not a single shot was fired.

108
00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:47.200
But the ledger was riddled with holes.

109
00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:51.650
The tool was first tried in nineteen fifty-eight, then shelved.

110
00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:54.820
Its stage was set in nineteen seventy-two.

111
00:10:54.820 --> 00:11:01.670
Iceland had announced that it was extending its fishing limit to fifty miles, effective the first of September, nineteen seventy-two.

112
00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:08.680
Britain and West Germany, relying on the nineteen sixty-one agreement, took the matter to the International Court of Justice;

113
00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:15.210
Iceland declared that it did not recognise the court's jurisdiction and did not even take part in the hearings.

114
00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:26.060
When the Court settled the case in nineteen seventy-four and ruled that the fifty-mile extension could not be invoked against the applicants, Reykjavik set the judgment aside without a flicker of concern.

115
00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:29.870
Events at sea had long since left the courtroom behind.

116
00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:42.320
On the morning of the fifth of September, nineteen seventy-two, the coast guard vessel Ægir — Ayir — under the command of Captain Gudmundur Kjaernested, came upon a black-hulled trawler with its number painted over,

117
00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.970
working a bank off the north-east of the island.

118
00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:49.460
The time was twenty-five past ten.

119
00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:54.070
She was asked her name by radio; the trawler's skipper gave no answer.

120
00:11:54.070 --> 00:12:03.960
When the call was repeated, what came back over the radio was not a name but a piece of music: Rule, Britannia!, the tune long taken as the anthem of the Royal Navy.

121
00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:09.530
Fifteen minutes later, at twenty to eleven, the cutter went into the water for the first time.

122
00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:13.020
Ayir passed down the trawler's port side.

123
00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:19.550
Trailing some two hundred and ninety metres astern of the patrol vessel, the steel hook severed one of the trawl warps.

124
00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:24.320
The ship was identified afterwards from the radio traffic: the Peter Scott.

125
00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:27.970
What followed was the same scene repeated for a year.

126
00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:36.740
Icelandic patrol vessels would drive in among trawlers drawn up in box formation, cut the warps with sharp turns of the helm, and then pull away.

127
00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:40.230
Everything a navy is designed for was useless here.

128
00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:47.560
You could not open fire, because the vessel facing you belonged to an allied country, and a single hit meant political catastrophe.

129
00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:54.730
You could not impose a blockade, because blockading Iceland would mean handing NATO's northern flank to the Soviets.

130
00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:58.860
The only tactic left was to physically put yourself in the way.

131
00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:10.030
In the spring of nineteen seventy-three, British frigates entered the area directly on protection duty; over the course of the second phase of the conflict, thirty-two frigates served in rotation.

132
00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:17.520
Now the ships were cutting across one another's bows, closing off one another's sterns, passing at ever shorter range.

133
00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:20.050
Collisions became inevitable.

134
00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:23.700
Some were errors of handling; some were not.

135
00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:28.470
Steel hulls tore at each other, and gashes opened above the waterline.

136
00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:35.000
On the twenty-ninth of August, nineteen seventy-three, Ayir collided with the British frigate Apollo.

137
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:40.570
The Icelandic ship's hull was damaged, and the crew set about repairs while still at sea.

138
00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:47.740
As seawater flooded the compartment, the engineer doing the welding, Halldór Hallfredsson, was electrocuted and killed.

139
00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:53.950
His was the only death in three separate phases of conflict, across hundreds of warp cuttings and collisions.

140
00:13:53.950 --> 00:14:00.320
History did not record him as a casualty of war; it could not, because there was no declared war.

141
00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:05.730
But in Reykjavik his death was read as proof of how seriously the matter was being taken.

142
00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:12.100
Two months later, in November nineteen seventy-three, the two sides agreed on an interim arrangement.

143
00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:20.870
British trawlers could go on fishing inside the fifty-mile zone for another two years, but their annual catch would be capped at a hundred and thirty thousand tonnes.

144
00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:23.880
London saw it as a ceasefire.

145
00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:33.130
In reality it was a timetable for withdrawal with a ceiling attached, and the agreement was due to expire on the thirteenth of November, nineteen seventy-five.

146
00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:35.340
Everyone knew that date.

147
00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:39.150
Before that date arrived, Iceland took the next step.

148
00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:48.080
In July nineteen seventy-five, the government announced that it would extend the fishing zone to two hundred miles; the decision came into force in the autumn.

149
00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:51.090
The reasoning was not only economic.

150
00:14:51.090 --> 00:15:00.820
A report published that year by the Marine Research Institute of Iceland said that the cod stock was heading for collapse at current levels of fishing and that the catch had to be cut back severely.

151
00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:08.470
With that, Reykjavik gained the strongest argument it could have asked for: this was not greed, it was a rescue operation.

152
00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:19.160
Two hundred miles meant an expanse of sea around the island many times larger than the total land area of the British Isles, and it took in the entire livelihood of Britain's distant-water fleet.

153
00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:24.570
On the sixteenth of November, nineteen seventy-five, the third and harshest phase began.

154
00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:30.940
Britain deployed twenty-two frigates, along with supply ships, tugs and support vessels.

155
00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:38.270
On the Icelandic side there was once again the same handful of patrol boats: Ayir, Odin, Tor, Tir and Baldur.

156
00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:43.360
This time the use of the cutter did not let up, but the main tactic shifted to ramming.

157
00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:48.130
Over the third phase, fifty-five collision incidents were recorded in all.

158
00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:54.660
Patrol vessels would drive in under the frigates' sterns, and the frigates would try to shoulder them away from the trawlers.

159
00:15:54.660 --> 00:16:02.470
In the winter sea of the North Atlantic, in darkness, in six-metre waves, steel hulls closing to within metres of one another.

160
00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:14.120
The footage was broadcast on British television every evening, and an uneasy question was growing in the public mind: why could one of the oldest navies in the world get nowhere against a country with no army at all?

161
00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:17.450
Because the matter was not being settled at sea.

162
00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:21.740
The real card was in Reykjavik's hand, and its name was Keflavik.

163
00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:27.950
Iceland, despite having no army, was one of the founding members of NATO in nineteen forty-nine.

164
00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:30.480
The reason was geography.

165
00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:40.370
The island sat squarely in the middle of the sea passage between Greenland and Britain, and when Soviet submarines wanted to move out of their bases in the Arctic Ocean and into the Atlantic,

166
00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:43.220
they had to pass through that gap.

167
00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:49.430
The air base at Keflavik had been opened to American forces under the defence agreement of nineteen fifty-one,

168
00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:57.400
and the submarine-hunting aircraft that took off from it operated the most critical listening and surveillance network on the alliance's northern flank.

169
00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:02.650
In the words of one commander, Keflavik was the sentry box at the door of the Atlantic.

170
00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:10.460
The Icelandic government repeatedly raised the prospect of closing that base and, if necessary, leaving the alliance altogether.

171
00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:15.070
It may well have been a bluff; but Washington could not afford to call it.

172
00:17:15.070 --> 00:17:22.560
At a moment when Soviet naval activity in the Atlantic was at its peak, even the possibility of that base closing was unacceptable.

173
00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:27.970
And it was not forgotten that Iceland had sold fish to Moscow in the nineteen fifties.

174
00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:37.540
The arithmetic became simple: what Britain stood to gain was the livelihood of a few thousand fishermen; what it stood to lose was the strategic architecture of the North Atlantic.

175
00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:44.230
On the nineteenth of February, nineteen seventy-six, Reykjavik broke off diplomatic relations with London.

176
00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:50.760
It was the first time in the history of the alliance that two NATO member states had severed relations with one another.

177
00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:56.330
This was the official announcement that a dispute over fish had become a crisis for the alliance.

178
00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:59.020
What followed moved quickly.

179
00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:06.510
The Secretary General of NATO intervened personally, and the American administration increased its pressure on London.

180
00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:12.400
The British government was forced to accept that the military superiority it held could not be used politically.

181
00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:19.090
With the agreement signed in Oslo on the first of June, nineteen seventy-six, the third phase came to an end.

182
00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:24.500
The terms were plain: Britain was effectively recognising the two-hundred-mile zone.

183
00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:32.630
In return, no more than twenty-four trawlers at a time would be permitted to fish in the zone, with a total catch of thirty thousand tonnes.

184
00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:39.960
That permission would last six months and would expire of its own accord on the first of December, nineteen seventy-six.

185
00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:43.770
The frigates withdrew from Icelandic waters.

186
00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:47.420
To the British public this was presented as a compromise.

187
00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:50.430
It was not a compromise.

188
00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:52.640
It was a calendar.

189
00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:57.890
When the first of December, nineteen seventy-six, arrived, nothing happened.

190
00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:04.100
No guns were fired, no ceremony was held, no newspaper headline was written.

191
00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:06.790
The ships simply did not go back.

192
00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:15.880
The waters that distant-water trawlers sailing from Hull, Grimsby and Fleetwood had fished for three generations closed in law that morning, and never opened again.

193
00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:19.050
The effect was felt almost immediately.

194
00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:31.340
The distant-water fleet fed not only a port's ships but the entire economy around them: ice factories, net lofts, fish processing plants, salt merchants, hauliers, shipyards.

195
00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:34.990
When the voyages stopped, all of it stopped at once.

196
00:19:34.990 --> 00:19:45.360
In the fishing neighbourhoods of Hull, on streets where every house had a seafarer in it, unemployment stopped being an economic indicator and became something you could see from the pavement.

197
00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:52.210
Thousands of fishermen, having spent their lives doing one job, learned that the job no longer existed.

198
00:19:52.210 --> 00:20:00.500
Worse still, because most of them were not technically even counted as permanent employees of the vessel owners, they received no redundancy payments.

199
00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:06.070
A man of fifty who knew nothing but the sea was left to start again in a port town that had shut down.

200
00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:10.520
The struggle to win compensation for these men dragged on for decades.

201
00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:17.050
It was only in the early two thousands that payments were finally made to roughly one thousand three hundred former trawlermen.

202
00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:20.700
By then, many of them were no longer alive.

203
00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:26.110
On the Icelandic side, victory proved a more complicated gift than expected.

204
00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:35.520
Once the foreign trawlers were gone, the truth that emerged was this: the sea was not infinite, and the only party now capable of exhausting it was Iceland itself.

205
00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:40.770
The country had to limit its own fleet in order to protect the waters it had won.

206
00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:48.420
From nineteen eighty-four onwards, a system was built up in stages, based on transferable catch quotas assigned to each vessel.

207
00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:58.790
The system made the stocks manageable, but it brought with it a new social argument: once a quota became a form of property, the sea too became something that could be bought and sold.

208
00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:02.440
Iceland is still having that argument today.

209
00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:06.730
The real legacy, though, reached far beyond the North Atlantic.

210
00:21:06.730 --> 00:21:15.980
In the years when Iceland declared two hundred miles, no such limit had any standing in international law; even the International Court of Justice had ruled against Iceland.

211
00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:20.430
But what was actually happening at sea dragged the law along behind it.

212
00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:30.640
The principle Britain fought against in nineteen seventy-six was one it adopted itself at the beginning of nineteen seventy-seven, declaring a two-hundred-mile zone off its own coasts.

213
00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:34.610
In those same years, dozens of countries did the same.

214
00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:44.660
The United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, signed in nineteen eighty-two, turned the two-hundred-mile exclusive economic zone into a written rule worldwide.

215
00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:54.870
The legal foundation for the fact that the most productive stretches of the oceans are today considered to belong to coastal states — for those broad blue frames on the maps — comes from here.

216
00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:57.880
What achieved this was not a navy.

217
00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:06.970
It was the stubbornness of a country with the population of a small town and no army at all — stubborn enough to make the other side's interests impossible to sustain.

218
00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:11.420
Iceland won no battles; it never needed to.

219
00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:15.070
What it won was the ruin of the other side's arithmetic.

220
00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:21.120
Every moment a steel trawl warp was cut was not a military loss for London but an accounting entry;

221
00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:28.770
and every moment the possible closure of Keflavik came onto the table was an alliance being forced to reorder its own priorities.

222
00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:37.860
This eighteen-year struggle, remembered in history as the Cod Wars, shows that the balance of power is not always measured in tonnage and in the number of guns.

223
00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:42.630
Who can afford what matters just as much as who cannot afford to give something up.

224
00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:46.280
For Britain, fish was an industry.

225
00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:49.930
For Iceland, fish was the country itself.

226
00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:59.500
And when something is vital to one side and merely important to the other, the outcome is usually decided by the needs of the side for which it is vital.

227
00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:03.790
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

228
00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:07.120
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
